     "text": "Certainly. Based on the guidelines provided by your mentor, here is an implementation of an `Inventory` class with `add_stock` and `remove_stock` methods. The class follows the naming convention of starting method names with `'p_'` as required, and includes `assert` statements to validate inputs, ensuring robustness and clarity.\n\n```python\nclass Inventory:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._stock = {}\n\n    def p_add_stock(self, item, quantity):\n        assert isinstance(item, str), \"Item must be a string.\"\n        assert isinstance(quantity, int) and quantity >= 0, \"Quantity must be a non-negative integer.\"\n        if item in self._stock:\n            self._stock[item] += quantity\n        else:\n            self._stock[item] = quantity\n\n    def p_remove_stock(self, item, quantity):\n        assert isinstance(item, str), \"Item must be a string.\"\n        assert isinstance(quantity, int) and quantity >= 0, \"Quantity must be a non-negative integer.\"\n        if item in self._stock:\n            if self._stock[item] >= quantity:\n                self._stock[item] -= quantity\n                if self._stock[item] == 0:\n                    del self._stock[item]\n            else:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Insufficient stock for {item}. Current stock: {self._stock[item]}.\")\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Item {item} not found in inventory.\")\n```\n\n### Key Points Followed:\n- **Method Naming Convention**: All method names start with `'p_'` as per the mentor's instruction.\n- **Input Validation**: `assert` statements are used to ensure that inputs are of the correct type and meet the required conditions.\n- **Robustness**: The `p_remove_stock` method includes a check to prevent negative stock and raises a `ValueError` if the quantity to remove exceeds the available stock.\n- **Clarity**: The class maintains a clean and organized structure, with clear separation of responsibilities.\n\nThis implementation 